1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,823
Put a 0.4 V step on the q axis of our
motor,

2
00:00:03,863 --> 00:00:08,302
rotor locked, and the current climbs to 10
A, all on q.

3
00:00:08,342 --> 00:00:13,721
Now spin it at 1500 rpm, back-EMF
balanced, and add the same step.

4
00:00:13,761 --> 00:00:20,390
This time q settles at 6.2 A, and 4.9 A
appear on d, where we applied nothing.

5
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:25,193
Two voltage equations and a torque
equation explain both: the d–q model.

6
00:00:26,293 --> 00:00:28,465
First, Lesson 13's answers.

7
00:00:28,505 --> 00:00:37,649
One: a 10° angle error leaves cos 10°,
98.5 % of the torque, and 1.74 A on d.

8
00:00:37,689 --> 00:00:43,713
Two: Hall sensors without interpolation
are off by up to ±30°.

9
00:00:43,753 --> 00:00:53,307
Three: the back-EMF is DC: ωe λm on q,
4.08 V at 1500 rpm, and zero on d.

10
00:00:54,407 --> 00:00:57,384
Start with the physics: each phase's
voltage is its

11
00:00:57,424 --> 00:01:01,275
resistive drop plus the rate of change of
its flux linkage.

12
00:01:01,315 --> 00:01:05,169
It links the phase's own current, the
other phases' currents

13
00:01:05,209 --> 00:01:09,528
through mutual inductance, and the
magnet's share, λm cos θe.

14
00:01:09,568 --> 00:01:14,151
With the currents adding to zero, the
mutual part folds in:

15
00:01:14,191 --> 00:01:17,763
one inductance, L, 0.05 mH for our motor.

16
00:01:17,803 --> 00:01:24,507
As a space vector, the magnet's flux
becomes λm e^jθe, turning with the rotor.

17
00:01:25,606 --> 00:01:27,168
Now look from the rotor:

18
00:01:27,208 --> 00:01:31,439
every stator-frame vector is its dq
version, turned by θe.

19
00:01:31,479 --> 00:01:34,269
Differentiate that product, and two terms
appear.

20
00:01:34,309 --> 00:01:37,788
The first is the change you'd see riding
on the rotor.

21
00:01:37,828 --> 00:01:41,658
The second exists even if nothing changes
on the rotor:

22
00:01:41,698 --> 00:01:47,165
a vector turning at ωe moves at ωe times
its length, a quarter turn ahead.

23
00:01:47,205 --> 00:01:50,301
That's j ωe λ, the speed voltage.

24
00:01:50,341 --> 00:01:55,602
Undo the rotation, and in the rotor frame
the voltage is R i,

25
00:01:55,642 --> 00:01:58,228
plus dλ/dt, plus j ωe λ.

26
00:01:59,328 --> 00:02:06,145
Split the flux linkage: on d, Ld i_d plus
λm; on q, Lq i_q.

27
00:02:06,185 --> 00:02:10,304
Multiplying by j turns d into q, and q
into minus d.

28
00:02:10,344 --> 00:02:14,146
So the d equation gains minus ωe Lq i_q,

29
00:02:14,186 --> 00:02:18,869
and the q equation gains ωe times the d
flux.

30
00:02:18,909 --> 00:02:23,710
Its magnet part is Lesson 13's back-EMF.
The rest is cross-coupling:

31
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:29,306
current on one axis induces voltage on the
other, in proportion to speed.

32
00:02:29,346 --> 00:02:34,801
In an interior-magnet motor, Ld and Lq
differ, and each term keeps its own.

33
00:02:35,901 --> 00:02:39,821
Each equation is a circuit: the applied
voltage, a resistance,

34
00:02:39,861 --> 00:02:43,607
an inductance, and a source driven by the
other axis.

35
00:02:43,647 --> 00:02:46,823
At standstill, they're two separate RL

36
00:02:46,863 --> 00:02:51,117
circuits with time constant L over R: 1.25
ms.

37
00:02:51,157 --> 00:02:59,682
At 1500 rpm, the coupling, ωe L, is 31 mV
per amp, close to the 40 mΩ resistance.

38
00:02:59,722 --> 00:03:06,828
And q carries the back-EMF: 4.08 V, ten
times the resistive drop at 10 A.

39
00:03:07,928 --> 00:03:12,679
Now simulate the opening step, locked
rotor first:

40
00:03:12,719 --> 00:03:20,265
i_q rises as one minus e to the minus t
over τ, 63 % at 1.25 ms, then 10 A.

41
00:03:20,305 --> 00:03:24,322
i_d stays exactly zero: without speed,
nothing couples.

42
00:03:24,362 --> 00:03:27,368
At 1500 rpm, held by a dynamometer,

43
00:03:27,408 --> 00:03:34,122
ωe Lq i_q pushes i_d up as i_q rises; then
ωe Ld i_d pulls i_q back down.

44
00:03:34,162 --> 00:03:39,702
The current spirals in to 6.18 A on q and
4.86 on d,

45
00:03:39,742 --> 00:03:44,501
38° off the axis: the arctangent of ωe τ.

46
00:03:44,541 --> 00:03:49,394
Lesson 19's decoupling feed-forward
cancels exactly these terms.

47
00:03:50,494 --> 00:03:55,175
Power is three-halves of v_d i_d plus v_q
i_q: three phases,

48
00:03:55,215 --> 00:03:59,431
each carrying half of peak volts times
peak amps.

49
00:03:59,471 --> 00:04:03,514
Substitute the voltage equations, and the
power splits three ways:

50
00:04:03,554 --> 00:04:07,626
copper loss, stored magnetic energy, and
the speed voltages.

51
00:04:07,666 --> 00:04:10,485
Those can only leave as mechanical power.

52
00:04:10,525 --> 00:04:15,179
Divide by the shaft speed, ωe over p, and
out comes the torque:

53
00:04:15,219 --> 00:04:20,414
three-halves p, times λm i_q plus (Ld −
Lq) i_d i_q.

54
00:04:20,454 --> 00:04:24,640
Magnet torque, plus reluctance torque.

55
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:34,929
At 3000 rpm and 15 A: 197 W in, 13.5 W of
copper loss, and 184 W at the shaft.

56
00:04:36,029 --> 00:04:40,819
Torque accelerates the rotor, against
viscous and Coulomb friction and the load.

57
00:04:40,859 --> 00:04:45,470
Free the shaft and apply 1 V on q. The
current jumps to 13.5 A,

58
00:04:45,510 --> 00:04:50,373
the rotor accelerates, and the rising
back-EMF chokes it off.

59
00:04:50,413 --> 00:04:53,368
The speed overshoots to 427 rpm,

60
00:04:53,408 --> 00:04:59,945
i_q turns briefly negative to brake it,
and it settles at 366 rpm.

61
00:04:59,985 --> 00:05:04,568
The back-EMF acts as a damper, with a 1.2
ms time constant,

62
00:05:04,608 --> 00:05:09,731
as short as the electrical one; any real
load adds inertia.

63
00:05:10,256 --> 00:05:13,391
In steady state the derivatives vanish,

64
00:05:13,431 --> 00:05:19,163
and the voltage is a vector sum: R i, plus
j ωe L i, plus j ωe λm.

65
00:05:19,203 --> 00:05:24,797
At 3000 rpm and 15 A on q: 8.17 V of back-
EMF,

66
00:05:24,837 --> 00:05:33,649
0.6 V of R i along the current, and 0.94 V
of j ωe L i along minus d.

67
00:05:33,689 --> 00:05:38,371
The sum, 8.82 V, leads the current by
6.1°,

68
00:05:38,411 --> 00:05:43,712
well inside the 13.9 V a 24 V bus can
supply.

69
00:05:43,752 --> 00:05:49,279
Since dq values are phase amplitudes, it's
also the phasor diagram of phase a.

70
00:05:49,319 --> 00:05:57,717
Minus 10 A on d would lower v_q by only
0.63 V: keep that in mind for Lesson 21.

71
00:05:58,817 --> 00:06:00,952
Did the transformation hide a mistake?

72
00:06:00,992 --> 00:06:04,214
Solve the same motor in phase coordinates
instead.

73
00:06:04,254 --> 00:06:06,321
Use the interior-magnet variant,

74
00:06:06,361 --> 00:06:11,134
whose phase inductances vary at twice θe,
starting from standstill.

75
00:06:11,174 --> 00:06:15,013
Clarke and Park of its phase currents land
on the dq simulation,

76
00:06:15,053 --> 00:06:18,141
within a few nanoamps; the torques agree
too.

77
00:06:18,181 --> 00:06:23,939
The dq model isn't an approximation: it's
the same equations, seen from the rotor.

78
00:06:25,039 --> 00:06:27,309
Now its limits. Linear magnetics:

79
00:06:27,349 --> 00:06:31,138
real iron saturates, so Lq falls at high
current.

80
00:06:31,178 --> 00:06:35,342
Sinusoidal back-EMF: harmonics and cogging
return as ripple,

81
00:06:35,382 --> 00:06:38,404
at six times the electrical frequency.

82
00:06:38,444 --> 00:06:41,278
No iron loss: Lesson 4's hysteresis and
eddy

83
00:06:41,318 --> 00:06:44,551
currents take power the equations don't
see.

84
00:06:44,591 --> 00:06:47,890
Constant parameters: from 20 to 80 °C,

85
00:06:47,930 --> 00:06:54,265
copper resistance rises 24 %, and the
magnet's flux falls about 7 %.

86
00:06:54,305 --> 00:07:00,074
And no inverter: PWM ripple and dead time
come in Lessons 17 and 18.

87
00:07:01,174 --> 00:07:05,833
Your turn. The release folder has a script
that takes your motor's pole pairs,

88
00:07:05,873 --> 00:07:08,703
resistance, inductances and flux linkage.

89
00:07:08,743 --> 00:07:11,832
It simulates the step, measures the time
constant,

90
00:07:11,872 --> 00:07:14,610
and draws the phasor diagram to scale.

91
00:07:14,650 --> 00:07:23,421
For our motor, it prints 1.25 ms, 4.86 and
6.18 A, and 8.82 V.

92
00:07:24,521 --> 00:07:26,801
Three predictions before next time.

93
00:07:26,841 --> 00:07:32,280
One: at ωe = 1000 rad/s, with i_d = 0 and
i_q = 10 A,

94
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:36,749
in steady state, what are v_d and v_q?

95
00:07:37,649 --> 00:07:42,396
Two: what sets the electrical time
constant, and what is it for our motor?

96
00:07:43,296 --> 00:07:47,242
Three: how would you get R and L from a
locked-rotor DC step?

97
00:07:48,142 --> 00:07:50,339
Next time: parameter identification,

98
00:07:50,379 --> 00:07:53,861
measuring each of these parameters on the
bench.
